window.ResearchNotesData = {"understand":[{"id":"UNDERSTAND-1","title":"How are fires different in microgravity?","status":"draft","scope":"Qualitative research summary; no numerical simulation data","path":"context/understand/fires-in-microgravity.md","markdown":"---\nid: fires-in-microgravity\ntitle: \"How are fires different in microgravity?\"\ncategory: understand\nexperiments: []\nrelated_experiments: []\nstatus: draft\nreviewed_on: null\nevidence_scope: Qualitative research summary; no numerical simulation data\n---\n\n# How are fires different in microgravity?\nShape – On earth, buoyancy drives hot air upwards, leading to the formation of the usual teardrop shape. However, in space where gravity, and therefore buoyancy, is not present, the fire burns equally in all directions, forming a sphere in perfect conditions. \nTemperature - Due to the lack of convention currents for the same reason, scientists can create ‘cool flames’ which burn at much lower temperature than normal fire.\nHow are they caused?\n1.\tElectronic malfunction – short circuiting, overheating wires, and faulty batteries /other electrical components, can all lead to fires.\n2.\tOverheating equipment \n3.\tFlammable materials – due to the oxygen rich environment, combustion becomes more intense and easier. Example – In the Mir space station in 1997, a canister of lithium perchlorate (the chemical burned to produce oxygen in the station) burst into flame.\n","reviewNotes":"Team review needed: the wording about absent gravity and cool flames needs correction. Microgravity is free fall; reduced convection alone does not explain cool-flame chemistry. Check the Mir incident attribution against a primary source."},{"id":"UNDERSTAND-2","title":"Physics of combustion","status":"draft","scope":"Qualitative research summary; no numerical simulation data","path":"context/understand/physics-of-combustion.md","markdown":"---\nid: physics-of-combustion\ntitle: \"Physics of combustion\"\ncategory: understand\nexperiments: []\nrelated_experiments: []\nstatus: draft\nreviewed_on: null\nevidence_scope: Qualitative research summary; no numerical simulation data\n---\n\n# Arrhenius equation\nDescribes how temperature influences the rate constant of a chemical reaction:\n\n\n$k = A e^{-\\frac{E_a}{RT}}$\n\nwhere:\n\n$k$ = reaction rate constant\n\n$A$ = pre-exponential factor\n\n$E_a$ = Activation energy (J mol $^{-1}$)\n\n$R$ = Gas constant (8.314 J mol $^{-1}$ K $^{-1}$)\n\n# Physics of combustion\nFree radicals (an atom or molecule containing an unpaired electron) can make combustion extremely dangerous. They can react to create more radicals, a process called chain branching. The balance between radical production and radical removal influences whether combustion can sustain itself: essentially a never ending chain until a limit of radicals is reached.\n\nA fire detection system could potentially identify chemical intermediates associated with oxidation, rather than relying exclusively on visible light or smoke. A potential system could identify chemical intermediates released and associated with combustion, rather than relying on visible light or smoke.\n\nPyrolysis (Thermal Decomposition): When solid materials like wood, paper, plastics, or textiles are exposed to high heat (without catching fire), thermal energy breaks down their chemical bonds which releases flammable gases and organic vapours (such as methane, hydrocarbons, or tars) from the surface. This leads to a chain reaction of decomposition which could create further fire hazards. It has no requirement of oxygen, so can happen before the fire actually catches: a material can begin producing combustible gases before it actually catches fire. If an electrical fault or another heat source causes a material to overheat, thermal decomposition can release gases before a developed flame exists. Instead of waiting for smoke, a detection system could investigate unusual volatile organic compounds (VOCs) associated with material degradation. However, normal spacecraft activities can also release VOCs, so the system would need to recognise abnormal combinations rather than treat every VOC measurement as evidence of fire.\n\n# Thermal decomposition is not the same for every material.\nDifferent polymers have different chemical structures and therefore, they produce different decomposition products. This should be taken into account when designing the system. \n\nThermal runaway occurs when a process generates heat faster than the system can remove it, causing temperature to rise and potentially accelerate further heat generation, creating a positive feedback loop.  \n\nRate of heat accumulation = Rate of heat generation - Rate of heat loss\n\nA fire detection system could monitor not just temperature, but the rate at which temperature is changing.\n\n| Stage | Process | Description |\n| ------ | ------ | ---------- |\n| 1 | Heating | Material absorbs thermal energy |\n| 2 | Thermal decomposition | Chemical structures begin breaking down |\n| 3 | Pyrolysis | Volatile decomposition products are released |\n| 4 | Oxidation | Fuel products react with oxygen |\n| 5 | Radical chain reactions | Reactive intermediates participate in combustion |\n| 6 | Ignition | Sustained combustion develops |\n\n# Flames surviving at lower oxygen levels\nLimiting oxygen index for certain droplet flames was significantly lower in reduced gravity than in normal gravity. \n\nFlex tests:\n\n__limiting oxygen index in microgravity__\nMethanol droplets: 12%\nn-Heptane droplets: 15%\n\nThis shows microgravity threshold was below the oxygen concentration necessary to sustain biological activity \n\nHowever, though they survive, they are significantly less dangerous, a result of their lower temperature. \n\nYou might expect this means microgravity reduces the risk associated with fire. However, oxygen isn’t the only factor in the equation. This is how combustion actually works.\n\n1. Heat from the flame reaches the liquid.\n\n2. The liquid evaporates, releasing fuel vapour. \n\n3. Fuel vapour moves outwards from the droplet. \n\n4. Oxygen moves towards the flame from the surrounding atmosphere. \n\n5. The fuel and oxygen react in a region surrounding the droplet. \n\n# Two types of self-extinguishing \n\n1. Diffusive extinguishing \n\n    Essentially just lack of oxygen. However, not very likely as diffusion works without gravity \n\n2. Radiative extinction \n\n    A flame emits thermal radiation, transferring energy away from the reaction zone. Eventually, heat losses exceed the energy being generated. \n\n\n# What does this have to do with anything?\n\nAs oxygen concentration decreased: \n\n- The droplet diameter associated with diffusive extinction increased. \n\n- The droplet diameter associated with radiative extinction decreased. \n\nTheir intersection showed the LOI. \n\nSo why do flames in microgravity survive at low oxygen levels? \n\n- Reduced buoyancy changes balance between heat generation and heat loss \n\n- Diffusion becomes more important \n\n- And fuel chemistry changes \n","reviewNotes":"The published FLEX summary supports approximately 12% methanol and 15% heptane LOI in oxygen/nitrogen, not universal or safe cabin limits. Diffusive extinction involves insufficient residence time, not just lack of oxygen. Do not repeat claims that lower-temperature flames are inherently less dangerous or that radical chains are never-ending. Pyrolysis is thermal decomposition; the six-stage list is conceptual rather than a mandatory sequence. The IIT diagram labels r_s as diameter but draws a radius. The page includes these clarifications."},{"id":"UNDERSTAND-3","title":"Flamelets","status":"draft","scope":"Qualitative research summary; no numerical simulation data","path":"context/understand/flamelets.md","markdown":"---\nid: flamelets\ntitle: \"Flamelets\"\ncategory: understand\nexperiments: []\nrelated_experiments: []\nstatus: draft\nreviewed_on: null\nevidence_scope: Qualitative research summary; no numerical simulation data\n---\n\n# Flamelets\nFlamelets occur in \"near-limit\" conditions (“in which the flame is forced into a transitional behaviour.)\n“Most previous studies of flame spread have been conducted in open environments, where the fuel and flame are not in proximity to other surfaces, and where the flow is strongly driven by buoyancy. This geometry represents a wall or various objects burning in a room or in the open environment. However, some recent experimental flame spread studies have treated constrained geometries and have demonstrated the appearance of complicated flame spread and flame behaviors [1], [2], [3]. These behaviors occur in what are referred to as “near-limit” conditions1 [4] in which the flame is forced into a transitional behavior. The type (or class) of flame spread examined in this work involves spread in very narrow gaps or channels. In narrow gaps, the flame becomes “near-limit” because it usually suffers large heat losses to the nearby walls. A second way to produce near-limit conditions is to reduce the oxygen inflow to levels low enough to starve the flame of oxygen.”\n\nThese \"near-limit\" conditions happen when:\n\nA: There is very constrained/confined geometric space (in close proximity to surfaces) (in the study, in an apparatus called a Narrow Channel Apparatus, NCA)\n\nOr\n\nB: \" A second way to produce near-limit conditions is to reduce the oxygen inflow to levels low enough to starve the flame of oxygen.\"\n\nThe NCA was an apparatus created in the study \" which allows well-controlled experiments to be conducted by varying three important variables in this regime: oxygen concentration, flow velocity, and heat loss.\" \" In this work, we also show via comparison with drop tower experiments that the NCA is well suited to study flame spread in simulated microgravity.\"\n\nDue to the narrowness of the NCA, most of the gravitational induced buoyant flow of the fire is suppressed and minimised, so it is like microgravity conditions, meaning the NCA can also be used for studying microgravity conditions at normal earth conditions.\n\n\"By “flamelet,” we mean small, 3-D flames that are formed when a wide, 2-D flame front weakens to the point where it can no longer be sustained, and breaks up into individual flames separated by distinct gaps of non-burning material [3].\"\n\n# Dangers of flamelets\nFlamelets are dangerous because of how small they are. Thus, when smoke is detected, it is very difficult to locate the source of the smoke, the fire.  \n\nTheir small size allows them to spread through narrow gaps most flames normally cannot spread too, thus allowing them to reach dangerous areas, where they can cause a much more dangerous fire to occur. \n\n\" One of these fires [flamelet fire], for instance, caused the crash of the 1998 Swissair Flight 111. The fire originated behind the cockpit bulkhead inside the in-flight entertainment system due to a wire arcing event. Metalized polyethylene terephthalate (MPET)-covered insulation blanket was ignited and a creeping flame spread along it in a narrow gap. The burn patterns on the recovered pieces were consistent with a small, weak flame that slowly spread driven by low velocity ventilation flows between the walls. Releases of smoke had the crew concerned, but they were unable to pinpoint the origin. Eventually, the near-limit flame breached a silicone vent cap, allowing a much greater airflow in the narrow gap, leading to a rapidly growing and spreading flame. By the time the crew realized the full extent of the fire, it was too late and soon thereafter the aircraft could no longer respond to commands from the cockpit. The flight crashed into the Atlantic Ocean while attempting an emergency landing at Halifax International Airport, killing all 229 passengers aboard [5].\n\nA creeping flame is a flame that moves in the opposite direction to gas flow. (This flow supplies fresh oxygen to the flame front while also removing combustion products, though it also exerts a cooling influence on the leading edge of the flame.)\n\nThe study tested 3 contributors to flamelet formation. Ambient oxygen concentration, flow velocity of the incoming oxidizer and heat loss (of the large flame) to a nearby object.\n\nReducing the oxygen concentration weakens the flame, making it more susceptible to quenching.\n\n(flames have been shown to preferentially spread upstream in a low velocity flow in microgravity, so the study uses a range of low opposed flows).\nIf the flow velocity is high enough, the flame front stays uniform. \nIf it is too low, the flame might not receive enough oxygen for combustion.\nIf the velocity is low enough, the flame might break into flamelet.\n\n","reviewNotes":"The quoted study and numbered citations need a complete reference. Narrow-channel behaviour is an analogy to some microgravity conditions, not a substitute for every space environment."}],"prevent":[{"id":"PREVENT-1","title":"Material selection and ventilation","status":"draft","scope":"Qualitative synthesis; no validated material rankings or airflow thresholds","path":"context/prevent/materials-and-ventilation.md","markdown":"---\nid: prevention-materials-ventilation\ntitle: Material selection and ventilation\ncategory: prevent\nexperiments: [SoFIE]\nrelated_experiments: []\nstatus: draft\nreviewed_on: null\nevidence_scope: Qualitative synthesis; no validated material rankings or airflow thresholds\n---\n\n# Materials and ignition\nHeating solids can cause pyrolysis, releasing gases and vapours. Material selection must account for the atmosphere and installed configuration. Composition, thickness, exposed area and nearby surfaces matter. Electrical faults and overheating remain potential ignition sources.\n\n# Ventilation\nAirflow carries heat, oxygen and smoke. In microgravity, ordinary buoyant rise is greatly reduced. Ventilation can supply oxygen to a fire as well as transport smoke to a detector. More airflow is not automatically safer. No universal safe fan speed or oxygen concentration follows from these descriptions. Vehicle-specific emergency procedures cannot be inferred from this page.\n\n# Evidence needed\nMaterial test results, atmosphere conditions, geometry, heat loads and measured airflow are needed for quantitative comparisons.\n\n# Existing project context\n- ../understand/physics-of-combustion.md\n- ../understand/flamelets.md\n- ../applications/applications.md\n- ../detect/laser-diode.md\n\n# Supplementary sources\n- NASA Fire Protection technical brief: https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/12/ochmo-tb-008-fire-protection.pdf\n- Flammability configuration analysis: https://standards.nasa.gov/standard/JSC/JSC-29353\n- Material testing and selection: https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20000034017\n","reviewNotes":""}],"detect":[{"id":"DETECT-1","title":"Laser-diode forward-scattering smoke detectors","status":"draft","scope":"Documented US ISS detector principle; not a current inventory of every ISS module","path":"context/detect/laser-diode.md","markdown":"---\nid: laser-diode-smoke-detector\ntitle: Laser-diode forward-scattering smoke detectors\ncategory: detect\nexperiments: []\nrelated_experiments: [same]\nstatus: draft\nreviewed_on: 2026-11-14\nevidence_scope: Documented US ISS detector principle; not a current inventory of every ISS module\n---\n\n# Laser-diode forward-scattering smoke detectors\n\n## Summary\nNASA describes the US ISS smoke-detector design as a near-infrared, laser-diode forward-scattering system. It measures light scattered by airborne particles rather than the ion current used in the Shuttle detector. Detector response depends on the smoke properties, so no single particle detector should be assumed equally sensitive to every overheating material. [S1, S2]\n\n## Definitions\n- **Laser diode:** a semiconductor device that produces laser light.\n- **Near-infrared (near-IR):** electromagnetic radiation just beyond visible red light.\n- **Scattering:** redirection of light by particles. This is the relevant description of the detector's measurement.\n- **Refraction:** a change in light's direction associated with a change in refractive index. It is not an adequate substitute for “scattering” in describing the measured smoke signal.\n- **Forward scattering:** scattered light travelling at an angle less than 90° from the original beam direction; 0° denotes the original direction.\n- **Photosensor / photodetector:** a component that converts received light into an electrical signal.\n- **Particle-size distribution:** the range of particle sizes and their relative abundance, rather than one size shared by every particle.\n- **Calibration:** establishing how an instrument signal relates to a known reference aerosol or quantity under specified conditions.\n\n## How optical smoke detection works\nA light source illuminates a sampling region. Particles redirect some light towards a photodetector positioned away from the direct beam. Electronics evaluate the resulting signal against calibrated alarm criteria. The presence of a single particle does not necessarily trigger an alarm. [S1]\n\nThis mechanism does not require an ionising radioactive source. An optical smoke detector detects particles illuminated by its own source; it is different from a flame detector that observes radiation emitted by a flame.\n\n## Why airflow and placement matter\nIn microgravity, smoke is not carried towards a ceiling by ordinary buoyant rise. Ventilation therefore strongly influences transport to a detector. NASA's fire-protection brief discusses equipment bays, cabins and ventilation-return ducts as relevant locations. Placement in an airflow path does not guarantee detection before smoke reaches the cabin. [S3]\n\n## Experimental evidence and limitations\nSAME exposed detectors to smoke generated from several spacecraft-relevant materials. The NIST/NASA account reports that larger smoke particles from cellulose, silicone and Pyrell were readily detected by the ISS photoelectric detector. This is a finding for the tested materials and conditions, not a universal sensitivity specification. [S2]\n\n- Scattering depends on particle size, composition, shape and concentration as well as the optical arrangement.\n- Dust and other non-fire aerosols can also scatter light.\n- Detector sensitivity and transport delay are separate: even a sensitive detector cannot respond to smoke that has not reached it.\n- This note supplies no validated signal-to-concentration conversion, alarm threshold or detection time.\n- Avoid saying that all ISS segments use identical hardware, or presenting historical publications as a verified current inventory.\n\n## Sources\n- **S1:** Urban et al. (2003), *“Smoke”: Characterization of Smoke Particulate for Spacecraft Fire Detection*, abstract and detector comparison. https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20040053578\n- **S2:** NIST (2018), *NIST/NASA Study Shows One Detector Doesn’t ‘Fit All’ for Smoke in Spacecraft*, SAME methods and detector-response discussion. https://www.nist.gov/news-events/news/2018/05/nistnasa-study-shows-one-detector-doesnt-fit-all-smoke-spacecraft\n- **S3:** NASA OCHMO (2023), *Fire Protection*, technical brief, slide 8, “Fire Detection”. This brief is explanatory guidance, not a replacement for applicable requirements. https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/12/ochmo-tb-008-fire-protection.pdf\n\n## Review status\nDefinitions and scope expanded; “refraction” corrected to “scattering”. Team review is still required.\n\n## Frequency and wavelength context\nNear-infrared radiation is invisible electromagnetic radiation with longer wavelengths and lower frequencies than visible red light. Frequency and vacuum wavelength obey **f = c / λ**, where f is measured in hertz, λ in metres and c = 299,792,458 m/s. This relationship does not establish the detector's operating wavelength; no exact instrument frequency is specified in this summary. Diagram colours make the invisible beam visible for explanation.\n","reviewNotes":""},{"id":"DETECT-2","title":"Brunswick incipient fire detectors","status":"draft","scope":"Historical Shuttle hardware; not current fleet configuration","path":"context/detect/brunswick-fire.md","markdown":"---\nid: brunswick-fire-detector\ntitle: Brunswick incipient fire detectors\ncategory: detect\nexperiments: []\nrelated_experiments: [same]\nstatus: draft\nreviewed_on: 2026-11-14\nevidence_scope: Historical Shuttle hardware; not current fleet configuration\n---\n\n# Brunswick incipient fire detectors\n\n## Summary\nThe Space Shuttle used actively sampled ionisation smoke detectors made by Brunswick. A pump drew air into the instrument, and an aerodynamic separator diverted larger particles away from its sensing chamber. Small particles entering the chamber changed an electrical current, providing an early indication of possible overheating or fire. This is historical hardware, not a statement about a spacecraft operating today. [S1, S2]\n\n## Definitions\n- **Incipient fire:** the early development of a potential fire; overheating can release detectable material before a visible flame appears.\n- **Pyrolysis:** chemical decomposition caused by heating. It is not synonymous with flaming combustion.\n- **Aerosol:** solid particles or liquid droplets suspended in a gas. Smoke is an aerosol; dust can also be an aerosol.\n- **Submicrometre:** smaller than one micrometre (µm). One µm is one millionth of a metre. Do not substitute “ultrafine”: that term conventionally refers to particles below approximately 0.1 µm.\n- **Ionisation:** the formation of electrically charged atoms or molecules.\n- **False alarm:** an alarm triggered without the fire condition the detector is intended to identify.\n\n## How the detector works\n1. A pump supplies a controlled air sample.\n2. The inlet flow divides into a sensing path and a bypass. Particle inertia and the flow geometry favour entry of smaller particles into the sensing path.\n3. Americium-241 produces ionisation inside the chamber. An electric field establishes an ion current.\n4. Aerosol particles attach to ions and change charge transport, reducing the current.\n5. Electronics evaluate the signal and its change against alarm criteria. [S1]\n\nThe separator is aerodynamic; it is not simply a mesh that catches dust. Its size selection cannot chemically identify a particle as smoke or dust. [S1]\n\n## Why microgravity matters\nA detector needs contaminated air to reach its inlet. Forced sampling helps draw air through the instrument, but does not guarantee collection from every part of a cabin. The Shuttle detector's small-particle emphasis also meant that its response depended on the smoke population produced by the source. [S2]\n\n## Limitations and interpretation\n- Do not describe this detector as admitting large combustion particles while rejecting smaller dust. That reverses the documented separation principle.\n- Particle-size discrimination can reduce nuisance responses; it cannot eliminate all false alarms or missed detections.\n- A particle signal does not identify the burning material, locate the source or measure toxic-gas concentration.\n- No calibration curve, alarm threshold or detection-time model is provided in this note. Do not calculate those values from this description.\n\n## Related experiment\nSAME compared spacecraft smoke detectors and characterised smoke from different materials. It was an investigation, not the name of the Brunswick instrument. See the optical-detector note for the comparison source.\n\n## Sources\n- **S1:** NASA, *Other Challenges in the Development …*, Space Shuttle Orbiter fire-protection discussion, printed p. 469, “Smoke Detector” and Figure 4 discussion. https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19850008615/downloads/19850008615.pdf\n- **S2:** Urban et al. (2008), *Spacecraft Fire Detection: Smoke Properties and Transport in Low-Gravity*, section “Space Shuttle Detectors”. https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/20080012612/downloads/20080012612.pdf\n\n## Review status\nMechanism checked against the sources above. Team review is still required before marking this note approved.\n","reviewNotes":""},{"id":"DETECT-3","title":"Multi-angle optical sensing and multi-gas monitoring","status":"draft","scope":"General sensing principles and documented research instruments; vehicle installation claims unverified","path":"context/detect/multi-angle-optical-sensors.md","markdown":"---\nid: multi-angle-and-multi-gas-detection\ntitle: Multi-angle optical sensing and multi-gas monitoring\ncategory: detect\nexperiments: []\nrelated_experiments: [saffire]\nstatus: draft\nreviewed_on: 2026-11-14\nevidence_scope: General sensing principles and documented research instruments; vehicle installation claims unverified\n---\n\n# Multi-angle optical sensing and multi-gas monitoring\n\n## Summary\nThese are two different measurement approaches. Multi-angle optical sensing compares light scattered by aerosol particles in different directions. Multi-gas monitoring measures selected gaseous compounds. They can supply complementary information, but a demonstration of either technology does not establish that a particular spacecraft uses it operationally.\n\n## Definitions\n- **Aerosol:** particles or droplets suspended in gas; distinct from individual gas molecules.\n- **Scattering angle:** angle between the incoming beam direction and the outgoing scattered light. Forward scattering is below 90°; backward scattering is above 90°.\n- **Signal ratio:** one measured signal divided by another, usually after calibration and background correction. It is not the number of sensors that have triggered.\n- **Refractive index:** a material property affecting how light propagates through and interacts with it.\n- **Absorption spectroscopy:** identification or measurement of substances through the wavelengths of light they absorb.\n- **Tunable diode laser absorption spectroscopy (TDLAS):** scanning a semiconductor laser over selected molecular absorption features and measuring the resulting loss of transmitted light.\n- **Detection limit:** the smallest signal or concentration distinguishable from background under stated measurement conditions.\n- **Smouldering:** flameless combustion of a solid; not synonymous with pyrolysis, which is thermal decomposition.\n- **ECLSS:** Environmental Control and Life Support System, the systems supporting a habitable spacecraft environment.\n- **IMV:** inter-module ventilation, movement of air between connected modules; the acronym alone does not establish any specific vehicle's duct or alarm configuration.\n\n## Multi-angle optical sensing: conceptual explanation\nMultiple photodetectors observe a particle sample at different scattering angles. Comparing their measured intensities can help characterise the aerosol when interpreted with an appropriate scattering model and calibration.\n\nThere is no universal conversion from a forward/backward signal ratio to “average particle size”. Wavelength, particle composition, shape and the size distribution affect the interpretation. Do not assume that small particles preferentially scatter backwards or that all large particles are smoke. Additional channels may aid discrimination; they do not guarantee elimination of false alarms.\n\nThis section is explanatory background, not a sourced specification of an Orion or Gateway instrument. No particular sensor angles, sizing algorithm or performance figures are established here.\n\n## Multi-gas monitoring: documented research\nA NASA/JPL record describes a six-channel Combustion Product Monitor (CPM), developed for Saffire, using tunable laser absorption to measure CO, HCl, HCN, HF, CO₂ and O₂. Its stated applications include combustion-event monitoring and post-fire cleanup assessment. Oxygen is monitored as an environmental constituent, not because it is itself a combustion product. [S1]\n\nA separate instrument paper describes five-channel calibration and testing for CO, HCl, HCN, HF and CO₂. These are instrument-development findings, not evidence that every operational spacecraft carries this monitor. [S2]\n\n## Gas names and what they mean\n| Formula | Name | Interpretation |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| CO | Carbon monoxide | A target combustion-related toxic gas; concentration alone does not locate its source. |\n| HCN | Hydrogen cyanide | A monitored toxic gas. Use uppercase HCN, not “HCn”. |\n| HCl | Hydrogen chloride | An acid gas targeted by the cited monitors. |\n| HF | Hydrogen fluoride | An acid gas targeted by the cited monitors. |\n| CO₂ | Carbon dioxide | Also produced by normal respiration; its presence alone does not establish fire. |\n| O₂ | Oxygen | An atmospheric constituent important to combustion conditions. |\n\nDo not infer which gases a particular material emits from this target list. Such attribution requires material-specific evidence. In particular, the original statement linking Teflon directly with HCN was not established by the supplied research.\n\n## How absorption measurements are interpreted\nA detector measures light transmitted through a gas sample near a selected absorption feature. Concentration estimation needs a calibrated relationship that accounts for optical path length, temperature, pressure and possible interference from other absorbing species. Different target gases require suitable spectral channels. “Absorbs infrared” does not mean a gas is automatically easy to detect at any concentration. [S2]\n\n## Orion and Gateway: evidence boundaries\nA 2009 Orion detector paper describes adapting commercial-aircraft smoke-detector technology for spaceflight. Its abstract does not establish the multi-angle geometry described in the original note. [S3]\n\nNASA's 2021 fire-safety roadmap lists Orion and Gateway detector development, but a roadmap does not demonstrate installed or operational hardware. [S4]\n\nThe following original claims remain **unverified and must not be presented as facts**:\n- Orion and Gateway both use the specific multi-angle and multi-gas arrangement described here.\n- Every pressurised Gateway module has the stated duct-mounted detector configuration.\n- Docking and IMV automatically provide shared early smoke detection between Orion and Gateway.\n\nThese claims need dated, vehicle-specific design or operations sources. Lack of verification here does not prove the claims false.\n\n## Dashboard interpretation\nUse this note to explain the distinction between particles and gases. Do not generate gas concentrations, detector efficiencies, alarm thresholds or clearance-to-enter decisions from the conceptual description. Any simulated outputs must be labelled separately from measured research results.\n\n## Sources\n- **S1:** Briggs et al. (2019), *The Combustion Product Monitor instrument for the spacecraft fire safety demonstration project*, NTRS abstract; record marked as a preprint. https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20210012108\n- **S2:** *Qualification of a Multi-Channel Infrared Laser Absorption Spectrometer for Monitoring CO, HCl, HCN, HF, and CO₂ Aboard Manned Spacecraft*, NTRS abstract. https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20170008204\n- **S3:** Sutin et al. (2009), *Smoke Detection for the Orion Crew Exploration Vehicle*, abstract, DOI 10.4271/2009-01-2542. https://saemobilus.sae.org/papers/smoke-detection-orion-crew-exploration-vehicle-2009-01-2542\n- **S4:** NASA (2021), *Development of Spacecraft Fire Safety*, slide 10, draft technology roadmap; notional mission information. https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/20210021318/downloads/Tech%20Dev%20for%20Spacecraft%20Fire%20Safety_Location_10-7-2021.pdf\n\n## Review status\nGeneral definitions added; deployment claims separated from supported research. Team review and an instrument-specific source for the multi-angle optical section are still required.\n\n## Diagram geometry and spectral context\nThe dashboard illustrates a forward sensor at **30° (acute)** and a backward sensor at **150° (obtuse)**, both measured from the incoming beam's forward direction at the scattering point. These are illustrative angles, not measured or verified instrument specifications. Sensor wavelength also affects scattering and calibration.\n\nInfrared absorption channels target selected molecular absorption features. Frequency and vacuum wavelength are related by **f = c / λ**, with f in hertz, λ in metres and c = 299,792,458 m/s. Longer wavelengths correspond to lower frequencies. Different target gases need suitable spectral channels; there is no universal gas-detection frequency. No numerical operating frequency is established here.\n","reviewNotes":""}],"suppress":[{"id":"SUPPRESS-1","title":"Fine water mist","status":"draft","scope":"Qualitative research summary; no numerical simulation data","path":"context/suppress/fire-water-mist.md","markdown":"---\nid: fire-water-mist\ntitle: \"Fine water mist\"\ncategory: suppress\nexperiments: []\nrelated_experiments: []\nstatus: draft\nreviewed_on: null\nevidence_scope: Qualitative research summary; no numerical simulation data\n---\n\n# Fine water mist\nThe ISS currently uses this. Roughly 6 pounds of ionised water is sealed within a flexible titanium metal bellows. Outside the bellows is 1.2 pounds of nitrogen gas stored at high pressure. When an astronaut squeezes the handle, the water is forced towards the nozzle regardless of the orientation of the extinguisher due to 0g. Just before exiting, the water and high-pressure nitrogen mix in a specialised Venturi nozzle. The now expanding nitrogen gas force the water apart into a super-fine mist of droplets of 10-50 micrometres wide. Because the water droplets are microscopic and spherical, their total surface area to volume ratios are high. The mist evaporates immediately into steam and absorbs massive amounts of thermal energy, dropping the temperature below combustion levels. Additionally, when the water particles evaporate, they expand in volume by up to 1,700 times. This cloud of team temporarily pushes oxygen away from the flame, starving it. This works both on and off spacecrafts.","reviewNotes":"The hardware quantities, droplet sizes and water terminology need source verification. This original draft is not an extinguisher specification or an operating procedure."},{"id":"SUPPRESS-2","title":"Electric field driven ionic wind","status":"draft","scope":"Qualitative research summary; no numerical simulation data","path":"context/suppress/electric-field-driven-ionic-wind.md","markdown":"---\nid: electric-field-driven-ionic-wind\ntitle: \"Electric field driven ionic wind\"\ncategory: suppress\nexperiments: []\nrelated_experiments: []\nstatus: draft\nreviewed_on: null\nevidence_scope: Qualitative research summary; no numerical simulation data\n---\n\n# Electric field driven ionic wind\nIonic wind is when high-velocity ions move between two electrodes, transferring their energy to the neutral air molecules through collisions that accelerate the gas in the direction of the ions. Ionic wind can be used to create air currents to control smoke movement and accelerate the extinction of certain types of flames.\n\nRather than using fans, ionic wind is silent and does not rely on moving parts, which could break. Additionally, ionic wind generators can be more lightweight and vastly more energy efficient. Ionic wind is good for controlling fires in micro-gravity environments, as it allows for air movement control without relying on convection currents and gravity. However, very high voltages are required, which can be dangerous. Research on using ionic wind for fire suppression is relatively new and experimental, \n","reviewNotes":"The efficiency and suitability comparisons need evidence for a defined apparatus. Gas motion can change flame behaviour in either direction; suppression is not guaranteed."},{"id":"SUPPRESS-3","title":"Acoustic fire suppression","status":"draft","scope":"Qualitative research summary; no numerical simulation data","path":"context/suppress/acoustic-fire-suppression.md","markdown":"---\nid: acoustic-fire-suppression\ntitle: \"Acoustic fire suppression\"\ncategory: suppress\nexperiments: []\nrelated_experiments: []\nstatus: draft\nreviewed_on: null\nevidence_scope: Qualitative research summary; no numerical simulation data\n---\n\n# Acoustic fire suppression\nExperiments on acoustic fire suppression (using sound to extinguish fires) have been carried out by NASA, but it remains strictly experimental. In acoustic fire suppression, low-frequency sound waves (around 20-80 Hz) are played with constant volume. The pressure waves vibrate and push oxygen molecules away from the fuel source to interrupt combustion. Obviously there is also no waste product or residual chemical from Acoustic fire suppression too. In 0g, low velocity winds draw oxygen into the flames, feeding and growing it. If the acoustic amplitude is too low to fully extinguish the fire, the induced air movement could intensify the burn instead of putting it out. Additionally, as the sound waves displace gas without cooling the fuel source, cool flames may reform from residual heat. \n\n","reviewNotes":"The NASA attribution and frequency range need a source. Sound moves the gas mixture, not oxygen selectively. Reignition and cool-flame chemistry need separate explanations."},{"id":"SUPPRESS-4","title":"Ventilation and flamelet extinction","status":"source-checked","scope":"Published experimental summaries; no raw run data imported","path":"context/suppress/ventilation-and-flamelets.md","markdown":"---\nid: ventilation-and-flamelets\ntitle: \"Ventilation and flamelet extinction\"\ncategory: suppress\nstatus: source-checked\nreviewed_on: 2026-11-15\nevidence_scope: Published experimental summaries; no raw run data imported\n---\n\n# Ventilation and flamelet extinction\n\nAirflow supplies oxygen and carries heat away. In NASA Glenn's confined-channel experiments, increasing airflow caused surviving flamelets to flare up and merge into a continuous flame front.\n\nThe demonstrated cooling sequence in Saffire V was **reduce flow → allow cooling → stop flow**. For PMMA samples V-3 and V-4, flow was reduced from 20 to 5 cm/s for 300 seconds. The samples extinguished immediately when the fan was then stopped. In the separate cotton test V-2, fire grew back when flow resumed after 70 seconds without forced flow. Surviving flamelets and embers were seen before imaging stopped; which sustained combustion throughout the remaining interval was not determined.\n\nBriefly opening ventilation and then shutting it off is a different proposal. These sources do not establish it as a reliable flamelet-extinction method. These experimental results describe specific fuels and conditions, not a cabin operating procedure. Ventilation here means forced internal airflow, not opening the cabin to vacuum.\n\n## Sources checked\n\n- [NASA Glenn: Fingering Flamelets Form a Dynamic Population](https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/20050217475/downloads/20050217475.pdf), pp. 1–2, text and figure caption: increased airflow produces flare-up and a continuous flame front.\n- [Urban et al., Fire Safety Implications of Preliminary Results from Saffire IV and V](https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/20210011521/downloads/ICES%202021-266%200503-2021%20final.pdf), ICES-2021-266, section III.A, pp. 4–6. Cross-checked the PMMA sequence against Figures 5–6 captions and material identity against Table 1. Cotton persistence is described beside Figure 3.\n\nContinue with [flamelet formation](../understand/flamelets.md) or [fine water mist](fire-water-mist.md). Dashboard section: [Suppress — ventilation](../../dist/suppress.html#ventilation).\n","reviewNotes":""}],"applications":[{"id":"APPLICATIONS-1","title":"Applications for earth","status":"draft","scope":"Source notes; evidence scope still to be reviewed","path":"context/applications/applications.md","markdown":"---\nid: applications-for-earth\ntitle: Applications for earth\ncategory: applications\nexperiments: [SoFIE, ACME, FLEX, FIRES]\nrelated_experiments: []\nstatus: draft\nreviewed_on: 2026-11-14\nevidence_scope: \n---\n\n# Finding fire-safe materials\nNASA's SoFIE experiments tested the flammability of plexiglass, cotton-based fabrics, and other materials commonly used in spaceflight. Although SoFIE's purpose was to study the safety of spacecraft fires, the data collected could improve fire safety on Earth by improving screening tests to evaluate fire-safe materials for homes, offices, aircraft, and other uses.\n\n# Reducing harmful combustion products\nOne ACME investigation called Flame Design aimed to study how much soot was produced by flames under different flame conditions. Soot is the solid carbon produced by flames when incomplete combustion occurs. It can cause environmental and health issues: it warms the atmosphere, contributing to global warming, and inhalation can lead to respiratory diseases and cardiovascular issues. However, there are situations where soot is desirable, such as in some culinary or industrial applications. The results of the investigation could aid in the design of flames that produce more or less soot and may help create more efficient and less polluting flames.\n\nFLEX led to the discovery of a type of flame called a cool flame, which continues to burn after the extinction of the visible flame in certain conditions. Different to regular flames, cool flames typically burn at about 400 °C and instead of producing carbon dioxide and water, cool flames produce carbon monoxide and formaldehyde. Cool flame research could lead to cleaner auto ignition through the use of HCCI (Homogeneous Charge Compression Ignition) engines. HCCI technology creates a less polluting combustion process, because it requires lower combustion temperatures, reducing the production of nitrogen oxides, and leads to more efficient combustion, using less fuel per mile.\n\n# Improvements to firefighting\nProject FIRES (Firefighter's Integrated Response Equipment System) aimed to apply advanced materials and design concepts from Apollo to improve firefighting gear that did not adequately protect from the dangers of fire suppression activity. Lightweight, fire-resistant, heat-protective materials originally developed for use in space suits and spacecraft can be used to create safer firefighting gear. Additionally, Scott Aviation and NASA's Johnson Space Center collaborated to develop lightweight firefighter's air tanks, only 20 pounds for a 30-minute supply.","reviewNotes":"Add primary references for the historical examples and distinguish proposed applications from demonstrated improvements."}],"experiments":[{"id":"EXPERIMENTS-1","title":"Experiments","status":"draft","scope":"Source notes; evidence scope still to be reviewed","path":"context/experiments/experiments.md","markdown":"---\nid: experiments\ntitle: Experiments\ncategory: experiments\nexperiments: [SoFIE, ACME, FLEX, FIRES]\nrelated_experiments: []\nstatus: draft\nreviewed_on: 2026-11-14\nevidence_scope: \n---\n\n# SoFIE\nNASA ran the Solid Fuel Ignition and Extinction project (SoFIE) from July 5, 2022 until February 2025. The project consisted of five experiments which aimed to improve understanding of how flames spread and materials burn in different environments.\n\n# ACME\nACME is a set of six investigations examining fuel efficiency and pollutant production in combustion and stands for Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments.\n\n# FLEX\nFlame Extinguishment Experiment (FLEX) Refers to a set of experiments conducted by NASA to study the behaviour of flames in microgravity. Lead to the discovery of cool flames and helped scientists better understand how to improve fire safety in a space station.\n\n# FIRES\nFIRES stands for Firefighter's Integrated Response Equipment System. It was a joint research and development program created by NASA and the US Fire Administration, meant to develop ultimate protective gear for firefighters to replace outdated gear.\n","reviewNotes":"Experiment descriptions are drafts. Verify dates and programme status against primary references. The catalogue still needs individual run records, conditions, measurements and links to underlying data."}]};
